Common Git & GitHub Errors Every DevOps Engineer Faces 🚀 

Working in DevOps means you live and breathe Git & GitHub daily but even experts hit common pitfalls.

 Here are some of the most frequent Git/GitHub issues and their real-world fixes 👇

1. What causes the error "fatal: not a git repository"?
 This happens when you run Git commands outside a Git-initialized directory.
 Answer: Navigate to the correct folder or initialize a repository using git init.

2. Why do we get "error: failed to push some refs to 'origin/main'"?
 It usually occurs when someone else pushed new commits to the same branch.
 Answer: Run git pull --rebase origin main before pushing again.

3. What is a merge conflict and why does it happen?
 Merge conflicts occur when two developers edit the same line in a file.
 Answer: Open the conflicted file, resolve the differences manually, then use git add . and git commit.

4. Why do we see "fatal: remote origin already exists"?
 You tried to add a remote that already exists in your Git configuration.
 Answer: Update it using git remote set-url origin <new-URL>.

5. What is a detached HEAD state in Git?
 It occurs when you check out a specific commit instead of a branch.
 Answer: Create a new branch from it using git checkout -b new-branch.
6. Why does Git show "permission denied"?
 SSH key authentication failed with GitHub.
 Answer: Generate a new SSH key using ssh-keygen and add it to your GitHub account.

7. What does "rejected non-fast-forward" mean?
 Your local branch is behind the remote branch.
 Answer: Run git fetch origin and then git merge or git pull --rebase.

8. Why does a large file push fail on GitHub?
 GitHub does not allow files larger than 100MB.
 Answer: Use Git LFS (Large File Storage) for large files.

9. Why do we see "fatal: unable to access ‘https://github.com/...’
 — SSL certificate problem"?
 It’s caused by SSL or proxy configuration issues.
 Answer: Check your proxy or temporarily disable SSL verification using
 git config --global http.sslVerify false.

10. What does "You have unstaged changes" or "Your branch is ahead/behind" mean?
 It means your working directory is not in sync with the latest commit.
 Answer: Run git status to check what needs to be committed or pulled.

11. Why do we get "Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by merge"?
 You have uncommitted local changes while trying to pull new updates.
 Answer: Commit or stash your changes using git stash before pulling.

12. What causes "error: src refspec main does not match any"?
 You are trying to push a branch that doesn’t exist or has no commits.
 Answer: Make sure your branch has at least one commit and verify the branch name.

13. Why does Git say "fatal: branch 'main' already exists"?
 You tried to create a branch that already exists locally.
 Answer: Switch to it using git checkout main or delete it if not needed.
